    "title": "Commandment wins Florida Derby for Brad Cox as Kentucky Derby nears",
    "text": "Updated March 28, 2026, 8:37 p.m. ETCommandment came from the back of the pack to win the Grade 1 Florida Derby in a photo finish at Gulfstream Park.The Brad Cox-trained horse earned 100 qualifying points to clinch a spot in the Kentucky Derby on May 2.The Puma, who earned 50 points for second place, is also expected to race in the Kentucky Derby at Churchill Downs.HALLANDALE BEACH, FL — Neither Brad Cox nor his son, Brodie — out of breath from cheering — was sure who won Saturday’s Florida Derby after a thrilling photo finish.“I’m not sure he got it,” Commandment trainer Brad Cox told a couple of reporters after watching on a television monitor in the Gulfstream Park saddling paddock.Commandment got it — and established himself as a key player for the Kentucky Derby in five weeks.With new rider Flavien Prat aboard, Commandment rallied from last place in a six-horse field and caught The Puma at the wire to win by a nose in the $1 million, Grade 1 Florida Derby.Buy your Better Derby Betting Tickets hereCommandment earned 100 qualifying points to clinch a spot in the Kentucky Derby on May 2 at Churchill Downs.“My mindset was already, ‘This is a good race, and it’s going to set him up for the (Kentucky) Derby, win or lose,’” said Cox, a Louisville native. “I’m glad we came out on the right end.”Commandment covered the 1 1/8 miles on a fast dirt track in 1:49.99 and paid $5.80 on a $2 win wager.The Puma, who earned 50 qualifying points, also is expected to head to the Kentucky Derby.“We thought we had him,” assistant trainer Gustavo Delgado Jr. said, “but other than that it was really, really exactly what we wanted to see before the (Kentucky) Derby.”Three long shots in the original nine-horse field scratched before the race — Albus, Redland Rebels and Gregarious.Long shot Wayne’s Law and Nearly, the 7-5 favorite trained by Todd Pletcher, battled on the lead early, setting fractions of 24.10 seconds for the quarter-mile, 48.80 for the half-mile and 1:13 for three-quarters.Meanwhile, Cox wasn’t thrilled about Commandment’s position.“Yeah, I wasn’t loving it,” Cox said. “Even on the far turn, it looked like the top flight was traveling well.”As the early leaders faded, The Puma took the lead and Commandment came charging.“I tipped him out turning for home, he swapped leads and from there he gave me a good, solid run,” Prat said. “I thought I had the (head) bob, but I wasn’t sure. … He showed some guts down the lane.”Prat was riding Commandment for the first time after Irad Ortiz Jr. chose to ride Kentucky Derby hopeful Renegade in Saturday’s Arkansas Derby at Oaklawn Park. Renegade won that race.Cox said he wasn’t concerned about the rider switch.“First thing Flavien said when he got back was, ‘He’s easy to ride,’” Cox said. “I said, ‘I told you.’ Everybody who gets on him says he’s full of class. He’s not tough. He doesn’t pull. He doesn’t overtrain himself. He actually just takes it easy. He has a great mind, and I think that’s going to take him a long way.”Owned by Wathnan Racing, Commandment improved to 4-0-0 in five lifetime starts and earned $613,800 for the victory. He was purchased for $500,000 at the 2024 Keeneland September Yearling Sale.Commandment has won three straight at Gulfstream Park — including the Mucho Macho Man on Jan. 3 and the Grade 2 Fountain of Youth on Feb. 28 — but now will head back to Churchill Downs at the site of his maiden victory last November.Cox said he’ll check the weather in Louisville before deciding when to ship Commandment to Churchill Downs.“I think he put to bed the question of how far he wants to go,” Cox said. “I think (winning) the 1 1/8 miles, the mile-and-a-quarter (of the Kentucky Derby), with the right trip, he can get it done.”Jason Frakes: 502-582-4046; jfrakes@courier-journal.com; follow on X @KentuckyDerbyCJ.",
